{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And also our livestock, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to mention this after he had already said to him, ״גַּם אַתָּה תִּתֵּן״? This would tell us that besides their taking their livestock — ״גַּם אַתָּה״ etc. — if so, he has already mentioned their livestock. And it would seem possible to say that he was concerned lest Pharaoh understand the phrase ״גַּם אַתָּה״ as referring not to their livestock but to the little ones, for besides the little ones going, he also needs to give, etc.; therefore he clarified the matter regarding the livestock. This is forced. Further, one needs to know the intent of his saying <b>כִּי מִמֶּנּוּ נִקַּח</b> etc. — what is the novelty in this? If it is to inform him that they serve HaShem through slaughtering sheep, behold he had already said to him, ״גַּם אַתָּה תִּתֵּן בְּיָדֵינוּ זְבָחִים״ etc.; if so there is no novelty in this information. Further, one needs to know his saying, ״לֹא נֵדַע מַה נַּעֲבֹד״ — for the plain sense of the words tells that by this he means regarding the estimate, that they do not know how heavy the service will be, and therefore they need their livestock and he too will give if he wishes; if so, he should have said ״כַּמָּה נַּעֲבֹד.״" "However, it appears that he intended to preclude one argument, lest Pharaoh make it — namely, that they would take the kind fit to slaughter to HaShem and leave behind the kind that is unfit. Therefore he said, ״וְגַם מִקְנֵנוּ יֵלֵךְ עִמָּנוּ״ — meaning, even those that are unfit for slaughter. And therefore he concluded, <b>לֹא תִשָּׁאֵר פַּרְסָה</b> — meaning, every animal with a hoof, and this includes horse and mule, camel, donkey, etc., for all of them are called mikneh. And his saying ״כִּי מִמֶּנּוּ נִקַּח״ is to be explained according to what they say in tractate Avodah Zarah (24b), and this is their language: ״Come and hear״ etc. ״אֲשֶׁר חָמַל הָעָם עַל מֵיטַב הַצֹּאן וְגוֹ׳ לְמַעַן זְבוֹחַ לַה׳״ etc. — what is ״mēṭab״? The value of the best. And what is the difference that he compressed the matter into buying? — end of quote. Behold that even though the verse says ״לְמַעַן זְבוֹחַ לַה׳,״ the Talmud explains it as לְמַעַן — in order to purchase sacrifices from their value; and likewise his saying here, <b>כִּי מִמֶּנּוּ נִקַּח</b>, is explained — meaning, even though they are unfit for slaughter, nevertheless we will need to make use of <b>מִמֶּנּוּ</b> — meaning, from their value we will take to serve HaShem, that is, needs of the service. And therefore he did not say ״מִמֶּנּוּ נִזְבַּח לַה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ.״" "And his saying <b>וְאַנְחַנוּ לֹא נֵדַע מַה נַּעֲבֹד</b> — meaning: And if you would say, let us estimate the amount sufficient for the service and the rest will be left behind, therefore he said ״לֹא נֵדַע מַה נַּעֲבֹד.״ The word ״מַה״ bears two meanings, and he intended both: the first is how much, and the second is what is the manner of the service that the El will command us, for it is possible that He will say to build a structure in which to slaughter, and gold vessels beyond reckoning, and precious stones and pearls, and other services. And even though they said ״וְנִזְבְּחָה״ etc., behold He also said ״שַׁלַּח עַמִּי וְיַעַבְדֻנִי״ (Exodus 4:23), and He introduced other particulars into this besides the slaughter; so that this matter of slaughter is not delimited as the sole thing — therefore we need everything we have, and ״גַּם אַתָּה תִּתֵּן.״" "And his saying <b>עַד בֹּאֵנוּ שָׁמָּה</b> — he was shrewd in answering, for Pharaoh might say to him: Are you not a prophet? Ask this thing of HaShem and the matter will be clarified, and what is unnecessary will be left behind. Therefore he said ״עַד בֹּאֵנוּ שָׁמָּה״ — meaning, that this is not within the discipline of asking our God about the matter to know what He will desire, until we arrive there, at the place of the service; then we will know the matter. Or perhaps, since Pharaoh did not accept this answer and suspected that Moses was cunning and deceiving him, answering with his own words with nothing, heaven forbid, truthful in his mouth — for presumably they know and are able to know, but Moses said this answer to his face of his own accord — therefore he became angry with him and said to him: ״אַל תּוֹסֶף רְאוֹת פָּנַי״ etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :register {:flags {3 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:26:50.060756203Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}